'''Lake Tahoe''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|t|ɑː|h|oʊ}}) is a [[Fresh water|freshwater]] [[lake]] in the [[Sierra Nevada]] of the [[Western United States]], straddling the border between [[California]] and [[Nevada]]. Lying at {{convert|6225|ft|abbr=on}} above sea level, Lake Tahoe is the largest [[alpine lake]] in [[North America]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Vancuren |first1=R. |last2=Pederson |first2=J. |last3=Lashgari |first3=A. |last4=Dolislager |first4=L. |last5=McCauley |first5=E. |title=Air pollution in the shore zone of a Large Alpine Lake – 1 – Road dust and urban aerosols at Lake Tahoe, California–Nevada |journal=Atmospheric Environment |date=2012 |volume=46 |page=607 |doi=10.1016/j.atmosenv.2009.12.001 |bibcode=2012AtmEn..46..607V }}</ref> and at {{convert|122160280|acre.ft|km3|1|abbr=on|lk=in}} it trails only the five [[Great Lakes]] as the [[List of lakes by volume|largest by volume]] in the United States. Its depth is {{convert|1645|ft|abbr=on}}, making it the [[List of lakes by depth|second deepest]] in the United States after [[Crater Lake]] in [[Oregon]] ({{convert|1949|ft|abbr=on|disp=or}}).<ref name="USGS Tahoe Facts">{{USGS |url = https://tahoe.usgs.gov/facts.html |archive-url = https://archive.today/20120604111854/http://tahoe.usgs.gov/facts.html |url-status = dead |archive-date = 2012-06-04 |title = Facts About Lake Tahoe |access-date = October 26, 2008 }}</ref>

The lake was formed about two million years ago as part of the Lake Tahoe Basin, and its present extent was shaped during the [[Quaternary glaciation|ice ages]]. It is known for the clarity of its water and the panorama of surrounding mountains on all sides.<ref>{{cite web |url = https://www.keeptahoeblue.org/protect/water |title = Water Quality |access-date = October 26, 2008 |publisher = The League To Save Lake Tahoe }}</ref> The area surrounding the lake is also referred to as Lake Tahoe, or simply Tahoe; its English name is derived from its [[Washo language|Washo]] name, {{Lang|was|Dáʔaw}}.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20131101231419/http://www.fs.usda.gov/Internet/FSE_DOCUMENTS/stelprdb5251066.pdf "The Washoe People" Past and Present]</ref> More than 75% of the lake's [[Drainage basin|watershed]] is [[National forest (United States)|national forest]] land, covered by the [[Lake Tahoe Basin Management Unit]] of the [[United States Forest Service]].

Lake Tahoe is a major tourist attraction in both Nevada and California. It is home to winter sports, summer outdoor recreation, and scenery enjoyed throughout the year. Snow and ski resorts are a significant part of the area's economy and reputation.<ref name="porterstahoe">{{cite web |url = http://www.porterstahoe.com/lake-tahoe-resorts.asp |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120102130319/http://www.porterstahoe.com/lake-tahoe-resorts.asp |archive-date = January 2, 2012 |title = Lake Tahoe Resorts Winter sports |access-date = October 26, 2008 |website = Porters Tahoe }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last = Munson |first = Jeff |title = In rocky economy, ski-resort jobs are seen as more than free passes |website = Nevada Appeal |date = October 21, 2008 |url = http://www.nevadaappeal.com/article/20081021/NEWS/810209953/1070 |access-date = October 29, 2008 }}</ref> The Nevada side also offers several lakeside casino resorts, with highways providing year-round access to the entire area.

==Toponym==
The name for Lake Tahoe derives from the [[Washoe language|Washo]] word for the lake, {{Lang|was|dáʔaw}}, meaning 'the lake.'<ref>{{Cite web|title = Lake Tahoe Basin Mgt Unit – History & Culture|url = https://www.fs.usda.gov/r05/laketahoebasin|website = www.fs.usda.gov|access-date = January 15, 2016}}</ref> Even though {{Lang|was|dáʔaw}} is used in the names of other lakes with modifiers (for example, {{Lang|was|áʔwaku dáʔaw}} for [[Pyramid Lake (Nevada)|Pyramid Lake]], meaning 'trout lake'), it often is used without a modifier to refer to Lake Tahoe. This may be because of Tahoe's importance to Washo culture.<ref>Natalie E. Davenport (2019). "Naming, Remembering, and Experiencing We' lmelt' iʔ [northern Washoe] Cultural Spaces in Wa she shu It Deh [Washoe Land]". ScholarWorks, University of Nevada, Reno. Accessed 1 Jan 2023. pp. 194, 329. https://scholarworks.unr.edu/bitstream/handle/11714/6780/Davenport_unr_0139D_13067.pdf</ref>

==Geography==
{{See also|List of Lake Tahoe peaks|Lake Tahoe-Nevada. State Park}}
Lake Tahoe is the second deepest lake in the U.S., with a maximum depth of {{convert|1645|ft}},<ref name="USGS Tahoe Facts"/><ref name="nps_lakes"/> trailing Oregon's [[Crater Lake]] at {{convert|1949|ft|abbr=on}}.<ref name="nps_lakes">{{cite web|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/World-s-Deepest-Lakes-2017-508.pdf|title=The World's Deepest Lakes|publisher=US Department of the Interior: National Park Service|access-date=October 31, 2008}}</ref> Tahoe is the 17th<ref>{{cite web |url= https://geology.com/records/deepest-lake.shtml |title=Deepest Lake in the World Deepest Lake in the United States |access-date=October 31, 2008 |publisher=Geology.com }}</ref> [[List of lakes by depth|deepest lake in the world]], and the sixth deepest in average depth. It is about {{convert|22|mi|abbr=on}} long and {{convert|12|mi|km|abbr=on}} wide and has {{convert|72|mi|abbr=on}} of shoreline and a surface area of {{convert|191|sqmi|km2}}. The lake is so large that its surface is noticeably convex due to the curvature of the Earth.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Peterson |first=Dave |date=2020-09-25 |title=How Much Does the Earth Curve? |url=https://www.themathdoctors.org/how-much-does-the-earth-curve/ |access-date=2023-07-15 |website=The Math Doctors |language=en-US}}</ref> At lake level the opposing shorelines are below the horizon at its widest parts; by nearly {{convert|100|ft|m}} at its maximum width, and by some {{convert|320|ft|m}} along its length.<ref>{{cite web |title=Distance to the Horizon Calculator |url=https://www.ringbell.co.uk/info/hdist.htm |access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Senesac |first1=David |title=Visual Line of Sight Calculations dependent on Earth's Curvature |url=https://www.davidsenesac.com/Information/line_of_sight.html |access-date=June 22, 2018}}</ref> Visibility may vary somewhat with [[atmospheric refraction]];<ref>{{cite web |last1=Young |first1=Andrew T. |title=Looming, Towering, Stooping, and Sinking |url=https://aty.sdsu.edu/mirages/mirsims/loom/loom.html |website=An Introduction to Green Flashes |publisher=San Diego State University |access-date=July 10, 2018}}</ref> when the air temperature is much greater than the lake temperature, [[Looming and similar refraction phenomena|looming]] may occur where the lake surface or opposing shoreline is lifted above the horizon. [[Fata Morgana (mirage)|Fata Morgana]] may be responsible for [[Tahoe Tessie]] sightings.

[[File:North Lake Tahoe Aerial photo Don Ramey Logan.jpg|thumb|left|North Lake Tahoe aerial photo]]
Approximately two-thirds of the shoreline is in California.<ref>{{cite press release |url=http://www.bluelaketahoe.com/media/release.php?p=p_display&l=2&t=1&id=128 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090222021159/http://www.bluelaketahoe.com/media/release.php?p=p_display&l=2&t=1&id=128 |archive-date=February 22, 2009 |title=Lake Tahoe Trivia |access-date=October 26, 2008 |publisher=Lake Tahoe Visitors Authority |date=June 10, 2005 }}</ref> The south shore is dominated by the lake's largest city, [[South Lake Tahoe, California]], which adjoins the town of [[Stateline, Nevada]], while [[Tahoe City, California]], is located on the lake's northwest shore. [[Kings Beach, California]], and [[Incline Village, Nevada]], anchor the lake's north shore.{{clarify|what does anchor the north shore mean?|date=January 2023}} Although highways run within sight of the lake shore for much of Tahoe's perimeter, many important{{clarify|in what way are these parts of the shoreline important?|date=January 2023}} parts of the shoreline lie within state parks or are protected by the [[United States Forest Service]]. The '''Lake Tahoe Watershed''' ([[USGS]] [[Hydrological code|Huc]] 18100200) of {{convert|505|sqmi|km2|abbr=on}} is the land area that drains to the lake and the Lake Tahoe drainage divide traverses the same general area as the [[Tahoe Rim Trail]].

[[File:Lake Tahoe, view of California from Nevada side.jpg|thumb|Lake Tahoe, view of California shore from Nevada side]]
Lake Tahoe is fed by 63 tributaries. These drain an area about the same size as the lake and produce half its water; the other half is produced by direct precipitation.

[[File:Sunrise over lake Tahoe.jpg|thumb|Sunrise over Lake Tahoe]]
[[File:Emerald Bay Oct 2023.jpg|thumb|Panorama of Emerald Bay (California) October 2023]]

The [[Truckee River]] is the lake's only outlet,<ref name="ltva2">{{cite web |title=Visit Lake Tahoe |url=https://visitlaketahoe.com/ |publisher=Lake Tahoe Visitors Authority}}</ref> flowing northeast through [[Reno, Nevada]], into [[Pyramid Lake (Nevada)|Pyramid Lake]] which has [[endorheic basin|no outlet]]. It accounts for one third of the water that leaves the lake, the rest evaporating from the lake's surface. Evaporation is thought to account for 40 to 60 percent of the water usage.<ref>Friedrich, Katja, et al. "Reservoir Evaporation in the Western United States: Current Science, Challenges, and Future Needs." ''Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society'', vol. 99, no. 1, 2018, p. 169. [https://www.jstor.org/stable/26639320 JSTOR website] Retrieved 5 June 2023.</ref> The flow of the Truckee River and the height of the lake are controlled by [[Lake Tahoe Dam]] at the outlet. The natural rim is at {{convert|6223|ft|abbr=on}} above sea level. The maximum legal limit to which the lake can be allowed to rise in order to store water is at {{convert|6,229.1|ft|abbr=on}}; a spillway at the dam controls overflow.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://waterdata.usgs.gov/usa/nwis/uv?site_no=10337000 |title=USGS – National Water Information System |access-date=April 14, 2016}}</ref> Around New Year 1996/1997 a [[Pineapple Express]] [[atmospheric river]] melted snow and caused the lake and river to overflow, [[1997 Nevada floods|inundating Reno and surrounding areas]].<!--two deaths--><ref>{{cite web|url= https://tahoequarterly.com/Features/Story/a-new-years-deluge |first=Matthew |last=Renda |title=A New Year's Deluge|date= December 2016 |access-date= December 22, 2016}}</ref>

==Natural history==

===Geology===
The '''Lake Tahoe Basin'''<!-- redirect target --> was formed by vertical motion (normal) [[Fault (geology)|faulting]]. Uplifted blocks created the [[Carson Range]] on the east and the main Sierra Nevada crest on the west. Down-dropping and block tilting (half-[[grabens]]) created the Lake Tahoe Basin in between.<ref name="USGS Tahoe Facts"/>  This kind of faulting is characteristic of the geology of the adjoining [[Great Basin]] to the east.

Lake Tahoe is the youngest of several extensional basins of the [[Walker Lane]] deformation zone that accommodates nearly {{cvt|12|mm|order=flip|}} per year of [[dextral shear]] between the [[Sierra Nevada-Great Valley Block]] and the [[North American plate|North America plate]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Oldow|first1=J.S.|first2=C.L.V.|last2=Aiken|title=Active displacement transfer and differential block motion within the central Walker Lane, western Great Basin |journal=Geology |date=January 2001 |volume=29 |pages=19–22|doi=10.1130/0091-7613(2001)029<0019:ADTADB>2.0.CO;2 |issue=1 |bibcode = 2001Geo....29...19O |last3=Hare|first3=J. L.|last4=Ferguson|first4=J. F.|last5=Hardyman|first5=R. F.}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Unruh |first1=Jeffrey |first2=James|last2=Humphrey|title=Transtensional model for the Sierra Nevada frontal fault system, eastern California |journal=Geology |date=April 2003 |volume=31 |pages=327–30 |doi=10.1130/0091-7613(2003)031<0327:TMFTSN>2.0.CO;2 |issue=4 |bibcode = 2003Geo....31..327U |last3=Barron |first3=Andrew }}</ref>
[[File:Tunnel Creek Road, Lake Tahoe, Nevada, 20220904, 02.jpg|thumb|Tunnel Creek Road, Lake Tahoe, Nevada (2022)]]
Three principal faults form the Lake Tahoe basin: the West Tahoe Fault, aligned between [[Meyers, California|Meyers]] and [[Tahoe City]], and which is the local segment of the [[Sierra Nevada Fault]], extending on shore north and south of these localities;<ref>{{cite news|title=New tool to dig up fresh quake clues|first=David|last=Perlman|newspaper=San Francisco Chronicle|date=September 3, 2012}}</ref>  the Stateline/North Tahoe Fault, starting in the middle of the lake and creating the relief that forms Stateline, NV; and the Incline Village Fault, which runs parallel to the Stateline/North Tahoe Fault offshore and into Incline Village.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/recenteqscanv/FaultMaps/120-39.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110607125612/http://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/recentescanv/FaultMaps/120-39.html |archive-date=June 7, 2011 |publisher=USGS |title=California-Nevada Fault Map centered at 39°N,120°W |access-date=September 18, 2012 }}</ref> The West Tahoe Fault appears to be the most active and potentially hazardous fault in the basin. A study in Fallen Leaf Lake, just south of Lake Tahoe, used seafloor mapping techniques to image evidence for paleoearthquakes on the West Tahoe and revealed the last earthquake occurred between 4,100 and 4,500 years ago.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Brothers|first1=D.S.|first2=G.M.|last2=Kent|title=New Constraints on Deformation, Slip Rate, and Timing of the Most Recent Earthquake on the West Tahoe-Dollar Point Fault, Lake Tahoe Basin, California |journal=Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America |date=April 2009 |volume=99|doi=10.1785/0120080135|pages=499–519|issue=2a|bibcode = 2009BuSSA..99..499B |last3=Driscoll|first3=N. W.|last4=Smith|first4=S. B.|last5=Karlin|first5=R.|last6=Dingler|first6=J. A.|last7=Harding|first7=A. J.|last8=Seitz|first8=G. G.|last9=Babcock|first9=J. M.|display-authors=4}}</ref> Subsequent studies revealed submarine landslides in Fallen Leaf Lake and Lake Tahoe, thought to have been triggered by earthquakes on the West Tahoe fault, and the timing of these events suggests a [[recurrence interval]] of 3,000–4,000 years.<ref>{{Cite journal|title = Paleoseismic history of the Fallen Leaf segment of the West Tahoe-Dollar Point fault reconstructed from slide deposits in the Lake Tahoe Basin, California-Nevada|journal = Geosphere|last1=Maloney|first1=J.M.|first2=P.J.|last2=Noble|first3=N.W.|last3=Driscoll|first4=G.M.|last4=Kent|first5=S.B.|last5=Smith|first6=G.C.|last6=Schmauder|first7=J.M.|last7=Babcock|first8=R.L.|last8=Baskin|first9=R.|last9=Karlin|first10=A.M.|last10=Kell|first11=G.G.|last11=Seitz|first12=S.|last12=Zimmerman|first13=J.A.|last13=Kleppe|display-authors=4|doi = 10.1130/GES00877.1|issue = 4|pages = 1065–90|volume = 9|year = 2013|bibcode = 2013Geosp...9.1065M|doi-access=free}}</ref>

Some of the highest peaks of the Lake Tahoe Basin that formed during the process of Lake Tahoe creation are [[Freel Peak]] at {{convert|10891|ft|m|0}}, [[Monument Peak (El Dorado County, California)|Monument Peak]] at {{convert|10067|ft|m|0}}, [[Pyramid Peak (El Dorado County, California)|Pyramid Peak]] at {{convert|9984|ft|m|0}} (in the [[Desolation Wilderness]]), and [[Mount Tallac]] at {{convert|9735|ft|m|0}}.<ref name="USGS Tahoe Facts"/> The north shore boasts three peaks at over {{convert|10,000|ft|sigfig=4}}: [[Mount Rose (Nevada)|Mount Rose]] at {{convert|10,785|ft|m|0}}, [[Mount Houghton]] at {{convert|10,490|ft|m|0}} and [[Relay Peak]] at {{convert|10,388|ft|m|0}}. Mt. Rose is a very popular hiking and backcountry skiing destination.

Eruptions from the now-extinct [[volcano]] [[Mount Pluto, California|Mount Pluto]] formed a [[volcanic dam]] on the north side. Melting snow filled the southern and lowest part of the basin to form the ancestral Lake Tahoe. Rain and runoff added additional water.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fs.fed.us/r5/ltbmu/faqs/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090510163228/http://www.fs.fed.us/r5/ltbmu/faqs/|archive-date=May 10, 2009|title=Frequently Asked Questions about Lake Tahoe and the Basin|website=Lake Tahoe Basin Management Unit|publisher=Forest Service, U.S. Department of Agriculture|access-date=June 20, 2007}}</ref>

The Sierra Nevada adjacent to Lake Tahoe were carved by scouring [[glacier]]s during the Ice Ages, which began a million or more years ago, and retreated ~15,000 years ago at the end of the [[Pleistocene]]. The glaciers carved canyons that are today iconic landmarks such as [[Emerald Bay State Park|Emerald Bay]], Cascade Lake, and [[Fallen Leaf Lake (California)|Fallen Leaf Lake]], among others. Lake Tahoe itself never held glaciers, but instead water is retained by damming [[Miocene]] volcanic deposits.

Soils of the basin come primarily from [[andesitic]] volcanic rocks and [[granodiorite]], with minor areas of [[metamorphic rock]]. Some of the valley bottoms and lower hill slopes are mantled with glacial [[moraine]]s, or [[glacial outwash]] material derived from the parent rock. Sandy soils, rock outcrops and rubble and stony [[colluvium]] account for over 70% of the land area in the basin. The basin soils (in the < 2&nbsp;mm fraction) are generally 65–85% sand (0.05–2.0&nbsp;mm).

Given the great depth of Lake Tahoe, and the locations of the normal faults in the deepest portions of the lake, modeling suggests that earthquakes on these faults can trigger [[tsunami]]s. Wave heights of these tsunamis are predicted to be on the order of {{convert|10|to|33|ft|0|abbr=on}} in height, capable of traversing the lake in just a few minutes.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Ichinose |first=G.A.|author2=Anderson, J.G. |author3=Satake, K. |author4=Schweickert, R.A. |author5= Lahren, M.M. |title=The potential hazard from tsunami and seiche waves generated by large earthquakes within Lake Tahoe, California-Nevada|date=April 2000|journal=Geophysical Research Letters |volume=27 |pages=1203–06 |doi=10.1029/1999GL011119 |issue=8 |bibcode=2000GeoRL..27.1203I |doi-access=free }}</ref>  A massive collapse of the western edge of the basin that formed McKinney Bay around 50,000 years ago is thought to have generated a tsunami/[[seiche|seiche wave]] with a height approaching {{convert|330|ft|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gardner |first1=J.V. |last2=Mayer |first2=Larry A. |last3=Hughs Clarke |first3=John E. |display-authors=1|title=The Lake Tahoe debris avalanche|journal=Morphology and Processes in Lake Tahoe (California-Nevada) Geological Society of America |date= 2000 |volume=112 |issue=5 |page=744 |doi=10.1130/0016-7606(2000)112<736:MAPILT>2.0.CO;2 }}</ref>

===Climate===
[[Image:Fallen Leaf Lake and Lake Tahoe South Shore.jpg|right|thumb|[[Fallen Leaf Lake]] and Lake Tahoe in background from Angora Ridge Rd. to the [[Angora Lakes]] Resort]]
Lake Tahoe has a [[dry-summer continental climate]] (''Dsb'' in the [[Köppen climate classification]]), featuring warm, dry summers and cold winters with heavy snowfall. Mean annual precipitation ranges from over {{convert|55|in|mm}} for watersheds on the west side of the basin to about {{convert|26|in|mm}} near the lake on the east side of the basin. Most of the precipitation falls as snow between November and April, although rainstorms combined with rapid snowmelt account for the largest floods. There is a pronounced annual runoff of snowmelt in late spring and early summer, the timing of which varies from year to year. In some years, summertime [[North American Monsoon|monsoon]] storms from the Great Basin bring intense rainfall, especially to high elevations on the northeast side of the basin.

August is normally the warmest month at the [[Lake Tahoe Airport]] (elevation {{convert|6254|ft|m|abbr=on|disp=semicolon}}) with an average maximum of {{convert|78.7|°F|°C|abbr=on}} and an average minimum of {{convert|39.8|°F|°C|abbr=on}}. January is the coolest month with an average maximum of {{convert|41.0|°F|°C|abbr=on}} and an average minimum of {{convert|15.1|°F|°C|abbr=on}}. The all-time maximum of {{convert|99|°F|°C|abbr=on}} was recorded on July 22, 1988. The all-time minimum of {{convert|-16|°F|°C|abbr=on}} was recorded on December 9, 1972. Temperatures exceed {{convert|90|°F|°C|abbr=on}} on an average of 2.0 days annually. Minimum temperatures of {{convert|32|°F|°C|abbr=on}} or lower occur on an average of 231.8 days annually, and minimum temperatures of {{convert|0|°F|°C|abbr=on}} or lower occur on an average of 7.6 days annually. Freezing temperatures have occurred in every month of the year.<ref>{{cite web
  | title =  Tahoe, California – Climate Summary
  | publisher=[[Desert Research Institute]]
  | url = http://www.wrcc.dri.edu/cgi-bin/cliMAIN.pl?ca8758
  | access-date =October 31, 2008 }} (1903–2007 climate data)
</ref><ref>{{cite web
